{# Successful fixed-N terminal report; included inside the clean-window scope.

   requested_count/completed_count are counted in units of work the window
   actually performed.  For the default infer probe that unit is an inference
   and the count is the host's fixed N.  For the busy_loop probe there are no
   inferences at all -- the window body is one calibrated spin sized from
   window_target_ms -- so exactly one unit is requested and one completes.

   Reporting clean_iters_n here under busy_loop is what shipped before, and it
   made the report self-contradictory: N requested against clean_count == 1
   completed, with status "complete".  collect_power_terminal.py reads that as
   "Power firmware reported incomplete inference execution" and raises, so the
   probe could never finish a run -- and elapsed_us, the number the probe
   exists to produce, was never consumed.  The host half of this agreement
   lives in power.diagnostics.expected_terminal_requested_count().

   The tags below are kept INLINE so the infer render stays byte-identical: the
   production Jinja env has trim_blocks off, so a control tag on its own line
   emits a blank line into the generated C (issue #119).

   Power binaries time their window with STIMER only. A DWT sum would be
   shorter than the gate rather than a bracket around it, and the gate and
   accumulation brackets read STIMER, so any other timer is a render error. #}{% if not use_stimer_window %}{{ {}["power binaries must time their clean window with STIMER"] }}{% endif %}
hpx_power_terminal_report(
    true,
{% if busy_loop_probe %}    1U,
{% else %}    (uint32_t)clean_iters_n,
{% endif %}    clean_count,
    clean_stimer_total_us,
    clean_stimer_gate_us,
    "complete",
    0U,
    {{ power_sync_enabled | lower }},
    true);
hpx_power_terminal_park();
